  "methodological_note": "We assemble two per-target per-seed AUROC matrices on the matched k=100-eligible 14-target cohort at the 10 canonical seeds (7, 13, 29, 42, 43, 44, 53, 71, 89, 97). The matched cohort k=0 baseline is taken from exp105_matched_cohort_k0_baseline.json (k0_per_target_matrix); the canonical 65-target k=0 LOTO baseline restricted to the same 14 targets is taken from exp_matched_cohort_baseline/baseline_k0.json (matched_cohort_14.per_target.<tgt>.per_seed), which is the canonical Morgan + RF LOTO run over all 65 eligible targets at the same 10 seeds, subset post-hoc. This yields 14 targets x 10 seeds = 140 paired observations. We compute the two-sided paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test (scipy.stats.wilcoxon, zero_method='wilcox') on these 140 pairs, the median of the paired differences, and a 95% percentile bootstrap confidence interval on the median paired difference using 5000 replicates of a target-clustered resample (sample 14 targets with replacement; pool the 10 paired differences for each selected target; take the median across the pooled set). RNG seed 20260502. Empirical finding: both protocols invoke leave-one-target-out with train_mask = ~test_mask over the full 65-target dataset and seed RNG per (seed, target) before fitting; restricting the held-out evaluation set to the 14 matched targets does not change the training set or the predictions, so the per-target per-seed AUROCs are bit-identical across the two cohorts. Consequently every paired difference is exactly 0.0, the signed-rank statistic is W = 0, the asymptotic p-value is undefined and is reported as p = 1.0 by convention, and the bootstrap 95% CI on the median paired difference collapses to [0.0, 0.0]. The matched-cohort k=0 baseline is therefore not merely within seed standard deviation of the canonical baseline -- the two are point-for-point identical -- and the manuscript Section 6.2 hedge can be strengthened accordingly."
